  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place across the region today \nthrough tonight. A short-wave disturbance aloft and weak cold \nfront will move across northern NY and New England today, which \ncould bring isolated showers to parts of the Adirondacks. \nHowever, moisture remains limited so much of the region will \nremain dry with a gradual increase in clouds. Highs this \nafternoon will be near normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter the recent stretch of dry weather, unsettled conditions \nare expected to arrive Wed P.M into early Thu as a progressive \nupper level trough and associated cold front move across the \nregion. Initially thhrough the day on Wed, showers will be \nconfined to areas north/west of Albany, closer to where the \nbetter forcing will be. Elsewhere, clouds will thicken and \nhumidity levels will increase, as dewpoints rise into the 60s.\n\nShowers are expected to then spread south/east across the rest \nof the area Wed night into early Thu A.M. as the cold front \nmoves east across the area. Due to increasing moisture (PWAT \nanomalies rise to +1 to +3 STDEV) some embedded downpours will \nlikely occur. Due to the progressive movement of the system and \nfairly strong winds aloft, flooding is not anticipated. Guidance\ncontinues to indicate little instability with MUCAPE < 500 J/Kg\nWed evening/night for areas north/west of Albany. Will continue\nto mention slight/low chance of thunder, but risk for severe \nstorms remains low. Due to strong flow aloft, SPC continues to \nindicate a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) of severe storms in the \nMohawk Valley and SW Adirondacks. This seems like a worst-case \nscenario though due to limited instability along with \nunfavorable time at night. NCAR AI NWP convective hazards \nforecast continues to show very minimal probs for any severe \nweather at < 5%. With strengthening SW winds aloft Wed into Wed \nnight (40-45 kt at 850 mb), occasional gusts of 20-30 mph will \noccur in favored S-N oriented valleys and southern facing \nslopes. \n\nAs the cold front pushes through mainly Thu morning, winds will\nshift to the W-NW with more widespread gusts of 20-30 mph \nexpected in a developing deeper mixing environment with cold \nadvection commencing. It will still be warm due to the mixing, \nalthough humidity levels will start to drop from NW to SE late \nin the day. A few lingering showers may occur along the front, \nespecially south of Albany.",
